  slides:
  - subtopicID: 0101
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Occupational Safety and Health (OSH)</b></size>\n\nIs a system designed to prevent workplace illnesses and injuries. It focuses on identifying hazards and assessing the risks they may cause. Everyone in the computer and technology industries is responsible for maintaining a safe workplace. Hazards should be eliminated or modified to reduce the risks they present. This module provides guidelines to help users and technicians understand health and safety responsibilities in the computer industry."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0101
    slideType: 0
    content: "There are three steps used to manage health and safety at work:\n\n1. Spot the Hazard (Hazard Identification)\n\n2. Assess the Risk (Risk Assessment)\n\n3. Make the Changes (Risk Control)\n\nAt work you can use these three Think Safe steps to help prevent\naccidents. Using the Think Safe Steps."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0102
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Common Hazards</b></size>\n\nA hazard is anything that could hurt you or someone else.\n\nExamples of workplace hazards include:\n\n• frayed electrical cords (could result in electrical shock)\n\n• boxes stacked precariously (they could fall on someone)\n\n• noisy machinery (could result in damage to your hearing)\n\nDuring work experience, you must remain alert to anything that may be dangerous. If you see, hear or smell anything odd, take note. If you think it could be a hazard, tell someone."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0102
    slideType: 1
    content: "<size=115%><b>Common Hazards</b></size>\n\nA hazard is anything that could hurt you or someone else.\n\nDuring work experience, you must remain alert to anything that may be dangerous. If you see, hear or smell anything odd, take note. If you think it could be a hazard, tell someone."
    image: {fileID: -1379164851, guid: 26accc8416e9f68419becb7f4528ad39, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0102
    slideType: 0
    content: "Assessing the risk means working out how likely it is that a hazard will harm someone and how serious the harm could be. Whenever you spot a hazard, assess the risk by asking yourself two questions:\n\n• How likely is it that the hazard could harm me or someone else?\n\n• How badly could I or someone else be harmed?"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "<size=115%><b>Physical Hazards</b></size>\n\nComputer technicians should be aware of hazards such as cables across floors, unstable equipment, and tools left on ladders. Temporary cables should be marked with warning signs or secured with gaffers tape. Permanent cables should be organized using cable ties, wraps, or raceways. Proper organization and secure equipment placement help prevent workplace accidents and injuries."
    image: {fileID: 397530054, guid: 0165cc06b85e5a34bb2d324f851d700b, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "<size=115%><b>Mechanical Hazards</b></size>\n\nWhen working with electronic equipment, always consider how it could cause injury. Be careful of moving parts, hot components, and sharp edges that can cause serious harm. Staying alert and handling equipment carefully can help prevent accidents and injuries."
    image: {fileID: 852546510, guid: 2eb36148423c36049a41bad6c14f2806, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "<size=115%><b>Chemical Hazards</b></size>\n\nChemicals used for electronic equipment can be harmful if swallowed or if they contact the skin or eyes. Always read and follow the warnings and instructions on chemical labels before use. Be especially careful when handling printer ink and toner, as they can stain skin, clothing, and surfaces."
    image: {fileID: 221726296, guid: 42e4763059ae8ca46aa4ee77249e7263, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "<size=115%><b>Electric Shock Hazard</b></size>\n\nComputers contain different voltages, but the power supply can have a dangerous 220 volts. Always unplug equipment before working inside it whenever possible. If you must work on powered equipment, remove jewelry and wristwatches to reduce the risk of electrical injury."
    image: {fileID: -813718166, guid: 8578f1bc93ea4cd48a5520dd7c525dd8, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0103
    slideType: 1
    content: "<size=115%><b>CRT Monitor High-Voltage Hazard</b></size>\n\nCRT monitors can be extremely dangerous because they contain high-voltage components that may hold a charge even after being unplugged. A CRT’s anode can carry up to 25,000 volts, so repairs should be handled by qualified professionals whenever possible. If opening one is unavoidable, proper discharge procedures must be followed to prevent electrical shock."
    image: {fileID: -1760798639, guid: a15350d8dff77ae4bbc9faea882c0b2e, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0104
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Occupational Health and Safety Standards</b></size>\n\nEveryone has a responsibility to report and address workplace hazards to protect others.\n\nProcedure:\n\n• Identify the hazard.\n\n• Clear the area around the hazard.\n\n• Partition or clearly mark the hazard area.\n\n• Safely clear the hazard if possible.\n\n• If it cannot be safely cleared, report it to the appropriate person.\n\n• Complete the proper documentation after the hazard is cleared.\n\nAll hazards should be reported using an Accident Report Form to help prevent future incidents."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0105
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Accident Reports</b></size>\n\nForms are used to give specific details with regards to the accidents happened in the laboratory during practical activities. Accident reports contain the following details:\n\n• Name of the person injured\n\n• Date and time of the accident\n\n• Type of injury\n\n• First aid given\n\n• Action taken to prevent further accidents"
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0106
    slideType: 1
    content: "<size=115%><b>Fire Exits</b></size>\n\nAll fire exits must be kept clear of obstacles such as chairs and boxes. Everyone is responsible for keeping fire exit doorways and nearby areas unobstructed. Corridors should have equipment stored on only one side to ensure a clear path during emergencies."
    image: {fileID: -1422242266, guid: c2de2cdf689aeb8468030add18e16159, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0107
    slideType: 1
    content: "<size=115%><b>Personal Safety While Working with PC’s</b></size>\n\nComputer equipment can cause serious injury or even death if proper safety guidelines are not followed. Before working with any computer equipment, technicians should take the necessary precautions to ensure their safety and the safety of others."
    image: {fileID: 1060451888, guid: 6a567d5d88d6fc84bba860d7f3334f10, type: 3}
  - subtopicID: 0107
    slideType: 0
    content: "The following are some precautionary measures to take before working with any computer equipment:\n\n• Always ground yourself before touching any part of the computer\n\n• Do not work alone\n\n• Be careful with tools that may cause short circuit\n\n• Replace only fuses with those proper ratings\n\n• Wear safety glasses for protection against sparks and metal fragmented\n\n• Use only grounded plugs and receptacles\n\n• Working area should have ventilations, trash can, fire exit and capable of being disinfect."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0107
    slideType: 0
    content: "• Keep one hand in your pocket when working live circuit.\n\n• Wear rubber sole shoes when standing on the ground or in a concrete floor.\n\n• Always power off and unplug the computer before working on it.\n\n• Wear hard hat when someone working above you\n\n• When making circuit changes, switch off and unplug the power cord from the equipment then discharge the capacitors."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0108
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Anti-Static and Safety Precautions</b></size>\n\nStatic electricity is a small electrical discharge that can occur when two objects have different electrical potentials. Although people may not feel static shocks until they reach several thousand volts, even less than 30 volts can damage sensitive computer components. Computer technicians must therefore protect equipment from static electricity to prevent damage. They should also take proper precautions to avoid electrical injury."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0108
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Safety and Anti-Static Rules</b></size>\n\n• When possible, try to avoid working in carpeted areas. Carpeting greatly increases static buildup within your body.\n\n• Always use an anti-static wrist strap when working on a computer (except when working on monitors)\n\n• Always disconnect a computer from the AC power and from any powered peripherals while you are working on it.\n\n• Always grasp a metal part of the computer chassis with your bare hand before you touch anything inside. Do this even if you are wearing an anti-static wristband."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0108
    slideType: 0
    content: "• Always handle electronic components by a non-conducting (nonmetallic) edge. Don't touch the pins or other connectors.\n\n• Never plug an ATX power supply into AC power unless it is connected either to a computer's motherboard or to a dummy test load.\n\n• Always use a UL-approved surge protector or an Uninterruptible Power Supply that incorporates surge and spike protection.\n\n• Never eat, drink, or smoke while working on a computer."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>The 5 'S' Process: Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke</b></size>\n\nThe 5S Process is a workplace organization system focused on cleanliness, efficiency, safety, and standardization. It was developed in Japan and is based on five Japanese terms: Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, and Shitsuke. In English, these are commonly represented as Sort, Set in Place, Shine, Standardize, and Sustain. A well-organized workplace can improve safety, productivity, worker morale, and responsibility."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Seiri</b></size>\n\nSeiri (Sort) is the first step of the 5S process, which involves removing unwanted, unnecessary, and unrelated items from the workplace. Only materials needed for work should remain, and even necessary items should be kept to a minimum. This helps simplify tasks, make better use of space, and avoid unnecessary purchases."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Seiton</b></size>\n\nSeiton (Set in Place) focuses on organizing items for efficiency. Every tool, material, and item should have an assigned place where it can be quickly accessed and returned. Proper organization improves workflow and worker productivity. Each location should also be clearly labeled for easy identification."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Seiso</b></size>\n\nSeiso (Shine) is the third step of 5S, which focuses on keeping the workplace clean. Everyone in the organization, from workers to managers, is responsible for cleaning their assigned areas. No area should be left uncleaned, and the workplace should always be kept presentable."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Seiketsu</b></size>\n\nSeiketsu (Standardize) is the fourth step of 5S, which focuses on creating standards for maintaining cleanliness and order. It includes keeping both the workplace and personnel clean and organized. Visual management, such as color-coding, helps identify problems or abnormalities easily. Workers should be trained to recognize and correct these issues immediately."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0109
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Shitsuke</b></size>\n\nShitsuke (Sustain/Discipline) is the fifth and final step of 5S, which focuses on maintaining orderliness and practicing the first four S consistently. It involves eliminating bad habits and developing good ones through regular practice. The goal is for workers to maintain cleanliness and organization voluntarily, without constant reminders from management."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0110
    slideType: 0
    content: "<size=115%><b>Make Yourself a Desktop Cleaner</b></size>\n\nHow to manage your desktop:\n\n<b>1. Create 5 folders in My Documents called:</b>\n\n• Inbox – Store unprocessed files that do not have a specific place yet, such as downloads and files from colleagues.\n\n• Action Items – Store files that require actions taking more than 2 minutes, such as forms and files to upload.\n\n• On Hold – Store items you are not ready to work on yet, such as articles, project ideas, and class information.\n\n• Current Projects – Store files related to active projects, with a separate folder for each project.\n\n• Archive – Store completed projects, reference materials, and files you may need again."
    image: {fileID: 0}
  - subtopicID: 0110
    slideType: 0
    content: "Tip: Adding an underscore (_) before a folder name makes it appear at the top of the list in My Documents.\n\n2. Move all relevant files from your desktop into these folders. If you have many files, move them all into the Inbox first and organize them later.\n\n3. Keep only program icons on the desktop to maintain a clean and organized workspace."
    image: {fileID: 0}
